Build it as a table-discovery + table-normalization agent, not just an Excel parser.

Core goal

Given any Excel workbook, detect every logical table separately, even when:

multiple tables exist on one sheet

tables are placed far apart

tables have merged cells

headers span multiple rows

blank rows/columns appear inside tables

small nested tables exist inside larger tables

titles/notes appear above or below tables

users format things visually instead of using Excel table objects


Best architecture

Excel file
   ↓
Workbook scanner
   ↓
Sheet layout grid
   ↓
Candidate table region detection
   ↓
Table boundary refinement
   ↓
Header/body/footer classification
   ↓
Nested table detection
   ↓
LLM/agent validation
   ↓
Structured output per table

Step 1: Read everything, not just values

Use openpyxl or python-calamine.

Extract for every cell:

row, column
value
formula
data type
merged-cell info
font bold/size
fill color
border
alignment
number format
row height / column width
hidden rows/cols

Formatting is very important because Excel tables are often visually defined.

Step 2: Convert sheet into a cell graph

Treat each non-empty or formatted cell as a node.

Edges connect nearby cells:

same row
same column
border-connected
aligned block
merged-region connected
nearby with small blank gap

Then detect connected components.

This gives you rough table-like islands.

Step 3: Detect candidate table regions

Use multiple signals together:

Strong signals

rectangular dense region of non-empty cells

consistent columns

borders around cells

bold/header-like top rows

repeated data types down columns

merged title row above body

similar row lengths

formulas repeated down a column


Weak signals

fill color

centered text

large font

currency/date/percentage formatting

blank row separating blocks


Each candidate region gets a score:

table_score =
  density_score
+ border_score
+ column_consistency_score
+ header_score
+ datatype_consistency_score
+ formatting_consistency_score
- prose_score
- random_notes_score

Step 4: Boundary refinement

After finding a rough region, expand/shrink boundaries.

Useful rules:

Expand if nearby row/column has:
- similar borders
- same alignment
- same number of populated columns
- formula continuation
- same fill pattern

Stop if:
- large blank gap
- different formatting block
- paragraph-like text
- title/note/footer
- another independent dense block starts

Allow small blank gaps inside a table, but not large gaps.

Step 5: Header detection

Headers are hard, so use both rules and LLM.

Possible header rows:

first 1–5 rows of the candidate table

rows with mostly text

bold/fill/center aligned

merged cells

rows before mostly numeric/date body begins


Then flatten multi-row headers:

Region | Q1 | Q1 | Q2 | Q2
       | Sales | Profit | Sales | Profit

becomes:

Region
Q1 Sales
Q1 Profit
Q2 Sales
Q2 Profit

Step 6: Detect nested tables

Inside a large detected region, look for subregions with:

separate border box

different header row

different column count

blank padding around it

distinct title

different fill/border style


Then decide:

if inner block is structurally independent:
    extract as separate nested table
else:
    keep as part of parent table

Step 7: Use LLM/agent as verifier, not first detector

Do not send the whole Excel blindly to an LLM.

Instead send compact metadata:

{
  "sheet": "Sheet1",
  "candidate_region": "B4:H22",
  "sample_values": "...",
  "format_summary": "...",
  "detected_headers": "...",
  "neighboring_cells": "...",
  "question": "Is this one table, multiple tables, or not a table?"
}

Ask the LLM to return strict JSON:

{
  "is_table": true,
  "table_type": "main|nested|summary|pivot-like|note",
  "final_range": "B4:H22",
  "header_rows": [4, 5],
  "body_start_row": 6,
  "footer_rows": [22],
  "confidence": 0.91,
  "reason": "Two header rows followed by consistent numeric columns."
}

Step 8: Output format

For every table:

{
  "table_id": "sheet1_table_003",
  "sheet": "Sheet1",
  "range": "B4:H22",
  "title": "Monthly Sales Summary",
  "header_rows": [4, 5],
  "columns": [
    "Region",
    "Q1 Sales",
    "Q1 Profit",
    "Q2 Sales",
    "Q2 Profit"
  ],
  "rows": [],
  "notes": [],
  "confidence": 0.91,
  "parent_table_id": null
}

Also produce CSV/Parquet per table.

Recommended implementation stack

openpyxl       → read workbook values + styles
pandas         → normalize extracted table data
networkx       → connected component detection
pydantic       → strict output schemas
LLM/agent      → table verification and header reasoning
rapidfuzz      → deduplicate similar headers/titles

Optional but powerful:

LibreOffice headless → render sheet to PDF/image
Computer vision      → detect visually boxed tables

Best practical approach

Use a hybrid system:

Rules detect candidates.
LLM validates and fixes boundaries.
Rules extract final data.
LLM fixes headers and table meaning.

Do not rely only on LLMs. Excel structure, coordinates, styles, merged cells, and formulas are deterministic and should be handled by code.

One-line strategy

Detect visual/structural table regions from the Excel grid first, then use an LLM agent only to resolve ambiguity, validate boundaries, identify headers, and split nested/nearby tables.